## a binop character other than '-' or '+' glued to ':=' starts a token that
## is not a valid identifier

x :=*2;  ##ERROR: syntax error - illegal identifier '*2'

---

## '=' glued to ':=' is the one case the lexer splits - the ':' is taken as a
## send colon, so what follows has to be a usable selector. '==1' is not one.

x :==1;  ##ERROR: syntax error - illegal identifier '==1'

---

## '|' opens a variable declaration once it is a token of its own

x :=|1;  ##ERROR: syntax error - invalid variable name '1'

---

## a method named '=' cannot be defined out of tree. ':=' is the assignment
## token, so 'K:=' is read as an assignment to K before anything else can look
## at it. 'K:==' works - see t/assign-01.hak - but a lone '=' does not.

class K: Object { }
fun K:=(a) { return 9 };  ##ERROR: syntax error - prohibited assignment around ':='
